we're taking a closer look at southeast  Asia today and how the Russian invasion  of Ukraine so far away is impacting the  region and also how some countries have  responded Asia has seen far fewer people  take to the streets to protest the war  nothing like the hundreds of thousands  who rallied across European capitals  instead most protests have involved  ukrainians and Russians abroad such as  this group in Bali Indonesia  Indonesian president Joko Widodo has  decided not to join the group of Nations  imposing sanctions on Russia in general  the country prefers to stay neutral when  it comes to conflicts in part because  the main domestic agenda is economic  growth and that means doing business  with as many countries as possible  Russia accounts for less than one  percent of trade with Indonesia but  about 16 percent of the country's  military weaponry is from Russia DW's  Georg Mattis had the chance to speak to  Indonesian Ambassador Nira sawajaya  director general for American and  European Affairs you can see how he's  trying to walk the line Ambassador Murad  How concerned are you about the  situation in Ukraine well we've been  very consistent from the beginning  before the war broke up we are already  sending a message that you know  everybody has to use the negotiation  peaceful settlement of dispute and  immediately when the attack began so the  president you know has already been  telling from his uh Twitter that you  know the war will will will not help and  also will end to the suffering of  humanity and that is the reason why we  are participating in the U.N General  Assembly so you mentioned the U.N  resolution for which Indonesia voted to  condemn the Russian invasion yes at what  point would sanctions be something that  Indonesia consider well you know what we  are trying to do is of course to stop  the conflict first and uh address the  humanitarian issues as well as create a  safe passage the safety of the civilians  is very important addressing the issue  of humanitarian is also very crucial and  that should be you know immediate step a  step that we have to take from an Asian  perspective what kind of geopolitical  lessons do you draw from this conflict  well you know very important lesson  first  conflict or military attack will not  resolve the issue will not benefit to  anyone and this is very important number  two the lesson that we learned  negotiation and peaceful settlement of  dispute should be the only options for  people to sit down number three I think  we have uh you know a stated uh many  times International Community is saying  that multilateralism should be upheld  should be strengthened do you think in  terms of world politics this will shift  the focus on the in the Pacific from the  United States for example rather towards  Russia again I hope not you know because  I think the International Community has  to work together in unity you know we  have very important agenda for Humanity  this is already affecting Humanity all  over the world we are talking about  pandemi we need to also collaborate in  unity we need to also address the issue  of economic reform post-pandemic  situations well of course the war should  stop but then you know we should not  also uh you know  forget that the International Community  has a very important agenda that we that  require us to be United to be in the  united front in order to address that  pandemic without that I think it will  keep on going and then it will also  bring uh you know uh you know tremendous  impact to humanity but but certainly I  think the message of the Indonesian  government is very clear the war should  stop Ambassador thank you very much for  your time thank you George thank you  not all countries in Southeast Asia have  stood on the sidelines Singapore often  viewed as Asia's version of Switzerland  surprised many when it decided to join  sanctions the foreign Ministry statement  made clear why quote the sovereignty  political Independence and territorial  Integrity of all countries big and small  must be respected Singapore takes any  violation of these core principles  seriously as they are fundamental to the  survival of Singapore a small state  joining us is Ian Chong associate  professor at the National University of  Singapore with a focus on international  relations in the indo-pacific Ian can  you tell us more about Singapore's  thinking what struck me was the blunt  assessment we're a small country we  worry about big countries  right so for Singapore it's a  long-standing issue about its size we're  small so there's a lot of emphasis on  international law on juridical equality  because the governing a party and in  fact others in society sort of see  having that sort of juridical equality  that protection of sovereignty as a  platform to raise Singapore standing to  give Singapore a sort of equality with  the larger Powers so that's certainly  going to be an emphasis in all all  things that Singapore does so before  when Singapore had to deal with the uh  arbitral tribunal ruling on the South  China Sea it also emphasized these same  sets of principles it's uh quite key to  Singapore's self-understanding and its  positioning internationally  but it's a country that has also  generally avoided taking sides so I  wonder how big a deal is this and do you  get a sense of whether citizens support  the move  right so  given um given the considerations of  size Singapore tends not to want to get  into big fights but because this is seen  um this issue of sovereignty and  international law is seen to be key to  Singapore's own interests so it's not  really taking a side uh apart from  Singapore's own side right so uh in  addition I think uh to the question  about you know how the public sees it  public actually is sort of split on it  there are some who are quite critical  they would rather just avoid any trouble  not say anything especially after Russia  had publicized uh Singapore's a list of  names that of countries that included  Singapore saying that they were  unfriendly to Russia so so there's some  fear of repercussion uh in addition I  think Singapore is talking uh about the  sovereignty issues but it's also  interested in joining the sanctions  because as a financial Hub in Southeast  Asia it probably has more links to  Russia and of course the rest of the  world than its neighbors in that sense  uh Singapore I think would be quite  concerned if any secondary sanctions  came and affected it that would look  really bad for Singapore's credibility  as and as this actor that tries to be an  honest broker that tries to emphasize  the rule of law so he doesn't want to  have that bad PR hit as well so  um I think that's part of the  consideration we can't rule out but of  course it sounds much nicer to talk  about uh these um you know more grounded  broader more abstract Noble even  principles  now we're starting to see how this  conflict will impact the global economy  the price of oil for example how do you  anticipate the global supply chain all  that stuff impacting Singapore  so Singapore even before the conflict  had been experiencing increasing  inflation like many other places of the  world in the world so this current sort  of spike in oil prices also in food  prices is going to hit Singapore I think  uh that the full effects aren't fully  aren't felt yet but they will bite and I  suppose it's a bullet that Singapore  will have to uh bite fortunately  Singapore  um is uh wealthier so it may be able to  you know come up with subsidies and  things like that for its own citizens  um although the slight wrinkle there is  that it also recently passed a goods and  services tax increase so uh some of its  citizens will really feel  um the pressure  um that come with inflation  Ian Chong thank you so much for joining  us  absolutely thank you  the clearest impact the sanctions have  of course is on Russians and that  doesn't just mean those in R